WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.680
Finish it right: the surgeon's knot and the two-step crimp.

2
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:12.160
This is the video for module two of Bead Making and Simple Crafts: guided practice with bracelets, necklaces and bead bags.

3
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:25.030
In beadwork, the finish decides the quality. Most homemade stretch bracelets break because the cord was not pre-stretched, the knot was wrong, or the knot sat against a sharp bead edge.

4
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.620
A crimp done well holds for years. Done badly, it slides open and drops every bead on the floor.

5
00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.920
In this video you will learn both finishes, and how to spot and fix the common faults.

6
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:45.400
Step one. Measure the wrist and add about 1.5 cm for 8 mm beads.

7
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.570
Step two. Cut about 30 cm of elastic.

8
00:00:50.820 --> 00:01:00.910
Step three. Hold each end and pull firmly, a few times, along the whole length. This takes out the first stretch, so the bracelet does not sag after a few days.

9
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.090
Step four. Thread your beads and check the length against the tape measure.

10
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:15.000
Step five. Tie the surgeon's knot. Cross right over left and wrap through twice. Then left over right, once. Pull hard.

11
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:30.630
Put one tiny dot of jewellery cement on the knot. Quick superglue can make elastic hard and brittle, and that is where it snaps. Slide the knot inside a bead, trim to about 3 mm, and let it dry flat for a few hours.

12
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.980
Funke in Ibadan has a customer with a 17 cm wrist who wants 10 mm wooden beads.

13
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:44.840
Because 10 mm beads are large, she adds about 2 cm instead of 1.5.

14
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.480
So the bracelet is 19 cm long.

15
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:58.410
19 cm is 190 mm. Divided by 10 mm per bead, that is 19 beads, on 1 mm elastic.

16
00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.180
Thread one crimp tube onto the wire, then the loop of the clasp.

17
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:14.270
Bring the short end back down through the tube, so both wires lie side by side. Leave a tail of about 3 cm and a small, loose loop around the clasp.

18
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:25.370
Place the tube in the back notch of the crimping pliers, the one with a dip, nearest the handle. Check the wires are not crossed, and squeeze. Now there is a groove.

19
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:32.450
Turn the tube a quarter turn, place it in the front notch, the round one, and squeeze to fold it in half.

20
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:44.960
Tug the wire and clasp hard. If anything moves, cut it off and redo it with a new crimp. Flattening a crimp with ordinary pliers is the mistake that drops beads on the floor.

21
00:02:46.210 --> 00:03:02.230
Before you crimp the second end, hold the necklace up in a curve. Leave a little ease, about the width of half a bead, so it drapes softly. Open jump rings by twisting one side towards you and one away. And put a smooth bead on each side of a sharp crystal.

22
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:15.360
Do not pull the wire so tight that the necklace stands stiff like a bangle. Do not pull rings open into a C shape, which weakens the metal. And do not let elastic rub on a sharp hole.

23
00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:22.120
Bead bags often use right-angle weave. Each unit is a small square of 4 beads.

24
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:29.850
Put a needle on each end of about 2 metres of line. Pick up 4 beads and cross the needles through the fourth bead.

25
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:37.570
Then pick up 2 beads on the right needle and 1 on the left, and cross again through the outer bead. Repeat.

26
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:45.920
Pull each cross firmly and evenly. If your units look like diamonds instead of squares, your pulls are uneven.

27
00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:54.570
Before a bag, weave a 5 by 5 test square and measure it. Then you can plan the size and buy the right number of beads.

28
00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:11.780
To remember. Pre-stretch elastic. Surgeon's knot: right over left twice, left over right once. Crimp in two steps, back notch then front notch, and tug-test every crimp. Leave a little ease. And always weave a test square before you plan a bag.

29
00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:25.720
Now practise. Make two stretch bracelets and compare them after five days. Practise five crimps on scrap wire. Make a princess necklace with matching earrings. And weave a test square, then a coaster.

30
00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.020
Continue with the lessons below.
